## 2. Evolution from the previous project

### 2.1 What the original prototype achieved

The attached `main.py` is approximately 600 lines and combines the complete system into one executable file. It defines:

- users and permission sets;
- data with separate authors and readers;
- primitive actions and nested LLM execution requests;
- the principal-intersection authorisation rule;
- recursive influence accumulation;
- nested-input readability;
- a defence interface;
- an exhaustive bounded evaluator;
- result categories for utility and prompt-injection outcomes;
- a hard-coded example environment.

Its central security rule is already present: an action is permitted only when every influencing author has the required permission. Nested executions add the authors of their inputs to the accumulated influencer set. The evaluator also saves and restores influence state around nested branches, demonstrating that branch-local influence was already understood as important. 

That is a meaningful research prototype. It contains the core of the later Conflux thesis rather than merely a demonstration script.

### 2.2 Limitations of the prototype

The main limitations were software-engineering and evaluation limitations rather than absence of a research idea.

The file combines domain objects, security policy, execution, exhaustive exploration, fixtures, metrics, debugging, and program startup. Importing it starts the evaluator. The environment, maximum depth, proposal cardinalities, and output format are hard-coded. Results are printed rather than returned as durable, versioned records.

The evaluator is also vulnerable to combinatorial explosion. It constructs every non-empty subset of data as a possible nested request, combines those requests with primitive actions, constructs proposal subsets, and explores bounded decision paths. This is useful for very small model checking, but it is not a scalable benchmark runner.

Several implementation details are fragile:

- `llm_calls_used` is assigned but unused.
- Helper functions such as `checker` and `any_auth` are unused.
- Set and frozenset iteration may make exploration and output ordering dependent on hash ordering.
- A 13-field count structure is later replaced with a 12-element list in one incomplete-task path.
- The first task-generation pass mutates the declarations list before the second pass uses it.
- One nested-task exact-match comparison appears to use the outer task’s data rather than the proposed nested task’s data.
- A no-match nested branch can calculate an index from `-1` values and then access a task-safety list through negative indexing.
- The numerous count positions make the evaluator difficult to review and easy to modify incorrectly.

These issues are normal for a research prototype, but they explain why directly extending `main.py` would have been a poor development strategy.

### 2.3 What has improved

The current repository has addressed many of these structural weaknesses.

Immutable dataclasses replace most hand-written equality and hashing. Artifacts explicitly contain values and provenance. Provenance supports composition and derivation operations. Execution state and traces have explicit types. Tests cover authorisation, provenance, policy boundaries, evaluation contracts, traces, ITES, and the normative MVP. The project is packaged using a `src` layout with strict mypy configuration, Ruff, pytest, and coverage tooling. ([GitHub](https://github.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/raw/refs/heads/main/src/conflux/core/provenance.py))

The original evaluator has also been conceptually decomposed into scenarios, task suites, benchmark runners, traces, reporting, statistics, counterexamples, environment suites, model benchmarks, and defence evaluation. This is the correct broad direction.

