## 3. Current repository architecture

### 3.1 Repository organisation

The repository now separates the system into packages covering:

- `core`: shared principals, permissions, provenance, artifacts, actions, resources, sessions, consent, and chat policy;
- `domain`: provider-neutral canonical domain types;
- `auth`: authorisation decisions;
- `ites`: the richer mediator and execution state;
- `research`: the normative minimal ITES implementation;
- `evaluation`: SLED-derived scenarios, traces, runners, reports, and benchmark services;
- `policy`: internal policies and the initial AWS adapter;
- `ports`: abstract environment, model, policy, resource, and tracing boundaries;
- `application`: use-case services;
- `adapters`: providers, model adapters, policies, and benchmarks;
- `compatibility`: staged legacy translation;
- `execution`: execution support.

The repository also has extensive architecture, development, evaluation, status, glossary, audit, ADR, and template documentation. ([GitHub](https://github.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/tree/main/docs))

This is a significant improvement over the prototype.

### 3.2 Canonical architecture versus implemented architecture

The main architectural caveat is that the clean architecture is not yet the sole implementation architecture.

The status document says that the domain, ports, application, and adapters packages currently provide typed boundaries over the validated implementation and that deeper migration is staged. Providers still consume compatibility-layer `Data` and `Environment` objects rather than the provider-neutral `EnvironmentSnapshot`. The application mediation service is currently a thin delegate around the existing ITES implementation. ([GitHub](https://github.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/blob/main/docs/STATUS.md))

This means Conflux presently has three overlapping layers:

1. legacy or SLED-derived implementation types;
2. canonical domain and port types;
3. compatibility translation between them.

That is reasonable during migration, but it increases the risk that the same concept acquires different semantics in different paths.

### 3.3 Dual ITES implementations

There are effectively two important ITES paths.

The normative `conflux.research.mvp` path covers:

- immutable artifacts and provenance;
- principal-context accumulation;
- primitive authorisation;
- nested execution;
- deterministic proposal ordering;
- bounded calls;
- explicit branch identifiers;
- sibling branch isolation;
- serialisable reports.

The richer mediator path adds:

- visible messages;
- consent;
- clarification;
- delegation;
- stop and no-op actions;
- sessions;
- provider-facing operations;
- compatibility hooks.

The normative semantics explicitly state that these richer features are extensions and are not part of the MVP semantics. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/refs/heads/main/docs/ITES_MVP_SEMANTICS.md))

This separation was useful while repairing the repository because it isolated the research claim from a larger implementation. It should not remain an indefinite architectural state. Every security-critical rule added to the richer path now risks diverging from the normative implementation.

The longer-term target should be:

- one semantic transition engine;
- extension policies layered over that engine;
- one shared trace model;
- conformance tests proving that restricted rich executions produce the same outcomes as the MVP.

