## 5. Evaluation and benchmark state

### 5.1 Native evaluation infrastructure

The repository has a credible foundation for evaluation:

- typed scenarios and task suites;
- benchmark orchestration;
- deterministic and real-model trace categories;
- counterexample and reporting types;
- model and system benchmark surfaces;
- one-shot and exhaustive evaluation separation;
- versioned trace concepts.

The evaluation documentation also correctly requires recording environment, attack, defence, model, provider configuration, seed, trace, metrics, and limitations. ([GitHub](https://github.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/blob/main/docs/EVALUATION.md))

The current benchmark-runner test, however, uses a synthetic one-task suite and a recording stub defence. It validates orchestration, not a real benchmark or defence outcome. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/refs/heads/main/tests/test_benchmark_runner.py))

### 5.2 Empirical results

The checked-in MVP results document is explicitly a template awaiting an executable run. It does not yet constitute an empirical result set. ([GitHub](https://github.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/blob/main/docs/MVP_RESULTS.md))

This is the most important project-level gap. The repository currently contains considerably more evaluation infrastructure than evaluation evidence.

Before adding new benchmark families, produce a reproducible baseline containing:

- a committed experiment manifest;
- a fixed Conflux commit;
- a fixed model or deterministic model definition;
- generated traces;
- raw results;
- aggregation script;
- tables and plots generated from raw results;
- documented exclusions and incomplete cases;
- an environment lockfile or container image;
- a machine-readable result schema.

### 5.3 External benchmark adapters

AgentDojo, CaMeL, and Dual-LLM adapters are substantial generic wrappers. They execute configurable external commands, capture stdout and stderr, parse JSON-like artifacts, and heuristically translate fields into Conflux traces. ([GitHub](https://github.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/raw/refs/heads/main/src/conflux/adapters/benchmarks/external/agentdojo.py))

They are not yet evidence of real integration with those systems.

For example, the AgentDojo wrapper assumes a configurable CLI accepting scenario identifiers, names, principals, data, tools, and optional output arguments. It then accepts several possible generic JSON field names. This is useful adapter infrastructure, but an actual integration needs:

- a pinned upstream repository and commit;
- an installation procedure;
- an exact supported upstream version;
- a concrete command;
- explicit scenario translation;
- an explicit trace schema;
- a fixture produced by the real upstream system;
- validation that Conflux’s task, attack, and success definitions match the upstream benchmark;
- failure handling that distinguishes benchmark failure from secure blocking;
- licence and redistribution documentation.

The repository status correctly says external adapters still require integration fixtures and documented assumptions. ([GitHub](https://github.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/blob/main/docs/STATUS.md))

### 5.4 Required experiment tracks

A defensible evaluation programme should have four tracks.

**Semantic model checking**

Use small exhaustive environments to verify invariants independently of language-model behaviour.

**Deterministic behavioural benchmarks**

Use scripted model outputs to test known attack and utility cases exactly and cheaply.

**Real-model benchmarks**

Measure security, utility, latency, token overhead, and variance across models and seeds.

**External comparative benchmarks**

Run ITES and competing systems under aligned tasks, tools, permissions, attacks, and outcome definitions.

Each track should report at least:

- unsafe action execution rate;
- data-exfiltration rate;
- benign task completion;
- false-block rate;
- incomplete execution rate;
- policy-decision agreement;
- calls, tokens, and latency;
- trace completeness;
- provider errors;
- results stratified by number and identity of influencing principals.

