## 7. Provider and production-execution state

### 7.1 Filesystem adapter

The filesystem adapter can enumerate a subtree, read a manifest, create resources and legacy data items, and perform read, write, delete, list, and stat operations. It checks resolved paths against the configured root, which is an important baseline control. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/refs/heads/main/src/conflux/adapters/providers/filesystem.py))

It is still an evaluation adapter rather than a production execution boundary. Production concerns include:

- time-of-check/time-of-use races;
- symlink replacement after validation;
- file ownership and operating-system ACLs;
- binary and large files;
- encoding errors;
- atomic writes;
- rollback;
- quotas;
- concurrent operations;
- immutable audit records;
- separation between the evaluator process and effectful provider credentials.

The adapter also catches broad exceptions and silently skips files during materialisation. That may make a benchmark environment appear safer or smaller than the real environment without making the omission visible.

### 7.2 Docker adapter

The Docker adapter inspects containers, reads optional manifests, materialises a compatibility environment, and supports inspect, logs, stop, exec, read-file, and write-file operations through the Docker CLI. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/refs/heads/main/src/conflux/adapters/providers/docker.py))

Several parts remain prototype-level:

- it imports legacy compatibility environment types;
- materialisation primarily describes the container rather than a complete resource graph;
- `docker.exec` derives the command from the permission name rather than a typed command payload;
- provider actions execute with whatever Docker authority the Conflux process possesses;
- there is no transaction, approval, capability-token, or dry-run layer;
- errors are converted to strings without a stable provider-error taxonomy.

Because Docker access often implies extensive host authority, this adapter must never be used as a production executor under the same privilege context as the model-facing process.

### 7.3 Recommended production execution model

Production side effects should be separated into a dedicated executor with:

- least-privilege credentials;
- typed and schema-validated requests;
- idempotency keys;
- precondition checks;
- policy snapshot identifiers;
- short-lived capability tokens;
- approval state;
- timeout and resource limits;
- transactional or compensating behaviour;
- tamper-evident audit events;
- tenant isolation;
- a narrow allowlisted operation registry.

The model and mediator should never directly hold broad cloud, filesystem, or container credentials.

