## 8. Testing and quality controls

### 8.1 Strengths

The repository uses:

- Python 3.12 or later;
- pytest and coverage;
- strict mypy;
- Ruff;
- immutable dataclasses;
- contract-oriented test files;
- architecture and terminology audits;
- a single local validation command.

The validation script runs the repository audit, pytest with coverage, Ruff, and strict mypy. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/refs/heads/main/scripts/validate.ps1))

The normative MVP tests cover mixed-context blocking, nested provenance accumulation, sibling isolation, shared call budgets, deterministic serialisation, and provenance creation. Those are valuable tests of research semantics. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/refs/heads/main/tests/test_ites_mvp.py))

### 8.2 Gaps

The visible test suite is mostly unit and contract testing. The external benchmark runner test uses a stub. I found no visible end-to-end tests executing the actual external defence repositories, Docker operations, or production-style policy snapshots. ([GitHub](https://github.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/tree/main/tests))

Important missing classes include:

- property-based tests;
- state-machine tests;
- differential policy tests;
- mutation testing;
- fuzzing of trace and adapter input;
- adversarial provenance tests;
- empty-context tests;
- multi-branch rich-mediator tests;
- provider race and failure tests;
- golden end-to-end traces;
- performance and state-space growth tests;
- benchmark reproducibility tests.

### 8.3 Audit script limitations

The custom repository audit is a useful Codex guardrail. It checks canonical documents, local links, module docstrings, audit-ledger coverage, preferred terminology, and selected dependency directions. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/refs/heads/main/scripts/audit_repository.py))

It is not a substitute for architectural tests. In particular, its dependency rules still reference paths and package names from before the recent provider and evaluation moves. For example, it checks a `src/conflux/providers` directory even though providers are now under `adapters/providers`, and several forbidden dependencies refer to old `sled`, `providers`, and `benchmarks` package names.

This is a concrete example of rapid AI-assisted refactoring outpacing its guardrails.

Use import-graph analysis or architecture tests against the current package names rather than text searches for selected import strings.

