## 9. Repository governance and delivery

As of the inspected snapshot, the repository had approximately 140 commits, one visible `main` branch, no open issues or pull requests, and no published release. GitHub Actions displayed the workflow-onboarding interface rather than a repository workflow. ([GitHub](https://github.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux))

This is adequate for personal experimentation, but not for a security-sensitive research artefact or future company-facing product.

Minimum governance additions should include:

- CI on every push and pull request;
- protected `main`;
- changes through short-lived branches;
- required passing tests, mypy, Ruff, audit, and coverage;
- a security policy;
- dependency and secret scanning;
- release tags;
- changelog entries;
- code-owner or reviewer rules for security-critical modules;
- reproducible benchmark workflows;
- retained CI result artifacts;
- issue templates for semantic defects and benchmark discrepancies.

Even as a solo developer, pull requests are useful because they create a review boundary for Codex-generated changes.

