## 10. Assessment of the Codex-assisted development cycle

### 10.1 Positive aspects

The repository is unusually well prepared for AI-assisted development.

`AGENTS.md` specifies security priorities, non-negotiable invariants, workflow expectations, and validation. The development guide requires inspecting architecture and APIs, writing a feature specification, making the smallest coherent change, validating, updating status, and auditing repository consistency. Templates exist for feature specifications and change checklists. ([GitHub](https://github.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/blob/main/AGENTS.md))

These are the correct mechanisms for using Codex on a security-sensitive project:

- persistent project instructions;
- explicit terminology;
- canonical documents;
- documented invariants;
- typed APIs;
- small changes;
- test and static-analysis gates;
- architecture decisions;
- an ownership ledger.

The recent commit messages are also much better structured than the earlier broad commits. They distinguish `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `docs`, and `chore`, and recent migration work was split into conceptually named commits. ([GitHub](https://github.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/commits/main/))

### 10.2 Observed development pattern

The history shows an initial file-generation phase, including separate modules for benchmark runners and external defences, followed by large folder reorganisations, documentation work, a preserved baseline, repair commits, clean-architecture boundaries, and a concentrated migration sequence on 26 July 2026. More than twenty commits are visible for that day alone. ([GitHub](https://github.com/RayaBuckley/Conflux/commits/main/))

This suggests the cycle has been:

1. generate broad feature scaffolding;
2. defer exact integration;
3. reorganise the package;
4. add documentation and AI instructions;
5. repair type and API inconsistencies;
6. add canonical boundaries;
7. migrate legacy code incrementally.

That has produced broad coverage quickly. It has also produced:

- duplicate or overlapping semantic paths;
- compatibility layers;
- generic integrations without real fixtures;
- documentation that can become stale immediately after refactoring;
- more interfaces than validated vertical features;
- a risk that “implemented” means “a plausible module exists”.

### 10.3 Main Codex risk

The largest risk is **plausibility without evidence**.

Files such as the external benchmark adapters are long, structured, typed, and professionally documented. They look complete. In practice, they remain generic wrappers until exercised against pinned upstream implementations.

The same pattern applies to production language. A provider adapter that can call `subprocess.run` is not yet a secure provider integration. A partial AWS policy evaluator is not yet real-world IAM support. An immutable trace type is not yet a reproducible audit trail.

Codex should therefore be asked to optimise for **evidence-producing vertical slices**, not repository breadth.

### 10.4 Recommended Codex task contract

Every substantial Codex task should begin with a committed specification containing:

- problem statement;
- security invariant;
- exact in-scope behaviour;
- explicit non-goals;
- canonical types affected;
- compatibility impact;
- failure modes;
- required tests;
- required trace evidence;
- acceptance commands;
- deletion or migration plan for superseded code.

A task should not be considered complete because code compiles. Completion should require one or more executable evidence artifacts.

For example:

> Integrate AgentDojo version X at commit Y, execute scenario Z, translate its actual output schema, retain the raw output fixture, produce a Conflux trace, and assert the expected security and utility result.

That is materially better than:

> Add AgentDojo support.

